// Title: Potential Network Sniffing Activity Using Network Tools
// Sigma ID: ba1f7802-adc7-48b4-9ecb-81e227fddfd5
// Status: test | Level: medium
// Author: Timur Zinniatullin, oscd.community, Nasreddine Bencherchali (Nextron Systems)
// Date: 2019-10-21 | Modified: 2023-02-20
// ATT&CK: credential-access, discovery, T1040
// Description: Detects potential network sniffing via use of network tools such as "tshark", "windump". Network
//   sniffing refers to using the network interface on a system to monitor or capture information
//   sent over a wired or wireless connection. An adversary may place a network interface into
//   promiscuous mode to passively access data in transit over the network, or use span ports to
//   capture a larger amount of data.
// False positives:
//   - Legitimate administration activity to troubleshoot network issues
// References:
//   - https://github.com/redcanaryco/atomic-red-team/blob/f339e7da7d05f6057fdfcdd3742bfcf365fee2a9/atomics/T1040/T1040.md
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/ccbb0369507f796db0b0ae6854c8a2be471b9d84/rules/windows/process_creation/proc_creation_win_susp_network_sniffing.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language 12.4.1, live Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where (FolderPath endswith "\\tshark.exe" and ProcessCommandLine contains "-i") or FolderPath endswith "\\windump.exe"
